WHAT THIS GRANT COVERS

The MIT grant above covers this repository's own work: the pipeline source in
`viz/src/`, the scene and pose-track data in `viz/scenes/`, the asset manifest,
and the rendered images and footage in `masters/`.

It does not extend to the third-party components listed below, which remain
under their own licences. Both are MIT-compatible; neither imposes a copyleft
obligation on this repository.

---

THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS

Two classes of third-party asset reach the published renders. Neither is
committed to this repository as bytes — the meshes are read from the sibling
`overboard` repo at build time, and the HDRIs and textures are fetched by
`viz/src/fetch_assets.py` — but both are *depicted* in the frames under
`masters/`, which are committed and public. The obligations therefore travel
with the renders, not merely with the source.

  Board meshes — enclosures, footpads, bumpers, electronics platform
      Openwheel, by Byte Sized Engineering
      https://github.com/bytesizedengineering/Openwheel
      MIT. ATTRIBUTION REQUIRED: the copyright notice must be retained in
      published material. See `sim/models/meshes/openwheel/LICENSE` in the
      `overboard` repo for the verbatim upstream text, and `NOTICE.md` in the
      same directory for attribution and for a documented README/LICENSE
      discrepancy upstream (the upstream README says GPLv3; the committed
      LICENSE file, which is authoritative, is MIT).

      The tire is NOT Openwheel-derived. Openwheel ships no tire mesh; the
      tire is a hand-authored MuJoCo primitive.

  HDRIs and PBR textures — lighting, ground, workbench surfaces
      Poly Haven — https://polyhaven.com
      CC0 1.0 Universal (public domain dedication). No attribution required
      and no restriction on use; recorded here for provenance rather than
      obligation.

`viz/assets/MANIFEST.json` is the authoritative per-file record — source, URL,
licence and sha256 for every external asset, with the reason it was chosen.
It is the compliance trail and it stays current.

---

WHAT THESE RENDERS ARE

Every image and clip in this repository is a **simulation**. The renderer
never computes physics: it replays motion that MuJoCo computed in the
`overboard` repo, and it takes deliberate visual liberties — lighting that is
not in the room, a tire profile nicer than the collision hull — precisely
because no control decision is ever tuned from a render.

No frame here is a photograph, and none depicts hardware that has been built
or ridden. As of this writing no hardware exists. Outdoor scenes are
deliberately anonymous: depicting an identifiable place would assert the board
had been taken there, which it has not.

Treating any frame in this repository as evidence of a working vehicle would
be a misreading of what it is.
